1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,677
An intern pushes a staging key to a branch
of her fork of your public repository.

2
00:00:09,717 --> 00:00:13,927
A fork is a second repository on
GitHub, hers, connected to yours.

3
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:17,341
Someone notices. The fork
is deleted within the hour.

4
00:00:17,381 --> 00:00:22,300
The CTO asks the question everyone hopes
has a short answer: is the key gone?

5
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:26,493
No. And the answer is worse than
"it might be cached somewhere".

6
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:31,480
The commit is reachable through your own
repository's URL, by anyone who knows its

7
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:36,408
ID. GitHub documents this behaviour. This
video shows you a local model of it, so

8
00:00:36,448 --> 00:00:41,274
that you understand the mechanism well
enough to act correctly in the first five

9
00:00:41,314 --> 00:00:45,289
minutes. Hold on to those five
minutes. They come back at the end.

10
00:00:47,129 --> 00:00:50,726
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:55,615
In the last video you learned to sort
everything into Git data and GitHub objects.

12
00:00:55,655 --> 00:00:59,908
A fork is the first place where that
sorting has a security consequence.

13
00:01:00,248 --> 00:01:05,957
We cover two short sections of Chapter 15.
Section 15.7, forks and the fork network,

14
00:01:05,997 --> 00:01:11,568
is the body of the video. Section 15.6,
stars and watchers, is a short addendum at

15
00:01:11,608 --> 00:01:17,248
the end of the concept, because forks, stars
and watchers are the three counters at

16
00:01:17,288 --> 00:01:21,285
the top of every repository
page and all three are misread.

17
00:01:21,625 --> 00:01:25,960
One replay, labs/ch15/fork-network.sh.
Be clear about what it is.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,670
GitHub documents the behaviour of
fork networks and not the mechanism.

19
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:36,121
Git has a documented mechanism of its own
with the same property, namespaces, and

20
00:01:36,161 --> 00:01:41,034
the demo uses it as a model. It is not
a claim about how GitHub is built.

21
00:01:41,374 --> 00:01:47,756
The model ends with git gc --prune=now,
🔴 DANGEROUS, on a throwaway repository.

22
00:01:47,796 --> 00:01:54,423
You know its five answers from Part 4. One
GitHub CLI command is discussed and not

23
00:01:54,463 --> 00:01:57,187
run: gh repo sync --force, also 🔴.

24
00:01:59,027 --> 00:02:00,439
After this video you can:

25
00:02:00,779 --> 00:02:05,123
Describe a fork as a separate repository
that shares object storage with its

26
00:02:05,163 --> 00:02:05,584
network.

27
00:02:05,924 --> 00:02:10,121
Show locally that a commit pushed to a
fork can be reached by ID through the

28
00:02:10,161 --> 00:02:10,623
upstream.

29
00:02:10,963 --> 00:02:14,191
Say what deleting a fork or a
branch does and does not remove.

30
00:02:14,531 --> 00:02:17,747
Explain what stars and watchers
are and are not evidence of.

31
00:02:19,587 --> 00:02:24,368
In one sentence: a fork is a second repository
on GitHub with its own refs, settings

32
00:02:24,408 --> 00:02:29,073
and permissions, connected to the repository
it was made from, and storing its Git

33
00:02:29,113 --> 00:02:30,336
data together with it.

34
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:35,556
Why do forks exist? So that people without
write access to a repository can publish

35
00:02:35,596 --> 00:02:40,299
commits somewhere and propose them. The
fork is theirs: their permissions, their

36
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:40,832
branches.

37
00:02:41,172 --> 00:02:43,684
What GitHub documents,
from its forks reference.

38
00:02:44,024 --> 00:02:49,492
One. A fork has its own branches, tags,
issues, pull requests, Actions, labels and

39
00:02:49,532 --> 00:02:55,069
wiki, and its own permissions. Forks of
public repositories are public and forks of

40
00:02:55,109 --> 00:03:00,510
private repositories are private. A fork's
visibility can't be changed by itself.

41
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:05,862
Two. A repository network is the upstream
repository, its forks, and forks of those

42
00:03:05,902 --> 00:03:09,696
forks. The upstream is the
repository the forks were made from.

43
00:03:09,736 --> 00:03:14,625
And then the sentence that matters:
"Repositories in the network share Git data."

44
00:03:14,665 --> 00:03:19,190
Commits pushed to any repository in a
network "can be accessible from other

45
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:24,059
repositories in that network, including
the upstream repository", and this holds

46
00:03:24,099 --> 00:03:29,110
"even after a fork is deleted". Owners of
an upstream repository can read all forks

47
00:03:29,150 --> 00:03:30,023
in the network.

48
00:03:30,363 --> 00:03:34,218
Three. Deleting a private repository
deletes its private forks.

49
00:03:34,258 --> 00:03:38,793
Deleting a public repository makes an
active public fork the new upstream.

50
00:03:38,833 --> 00:03:43,553
Removing a person's access to a private
repository deletes their forks of it.

51
00:03:43,893 --> 00:03:47,725
Four. Branch and tag rulesets
aren't inherited by forks.

52
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:53,257
Push rulesets apply to the whole network.
A ruleset is a named list of rules for

53
00:03:53,297 --> 00:03:54,086
chosen refs.

54
00:03:54,426 --> 00:04:00,118
Five. A deleted repository can be restored
within 90 days, unless its fork network

55
00:04:00,158 --> 00:04:01,026
is not empty.

56
00:04:01,366 --> 00:04:05,838
Inside .git, on your side: to your
clone a fork is one more remote.

57
00:04:05,878 --> 00:04:11,495
The parent-and-fork relationship is a GitHub
object that no ref or configuration key

58
00:04:11,535 --> 00:04:16,411
records. The upstream remote that gh
repo fork and gh repo clone add is a

59
00:04:16,451 --> 00:04:19,710
convenience in .git/config,
not the relationship.

60
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:24,860
Now connect this to Part 4. You know that
an object exists independently of the

61
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:29,157
refs, the names that reach it, and
that deleting a ref deletes a name.

62
00:04:29,197 --> 00:04:34,129
A fork network is that fact at the scale
of a platform: several sets of refs over

63
00:04:34,169 --> 00:04:37,935
one store of objects. Deleting
a fork deletes one set of refs.

64
00:04:38,275 --> 00:04:43,185
How long does an unreachable commit, one
that no ref reaches, remain on GitHub?

65
00:04:43,225 --> 00:04:48,197
The textbook is explicit about what is
not known: the course's research found no

66
00:04:48,237 --> 00:04:51,455
documented retention period
for unreachable commits.

67
00:04:51,495 --> 00:04:53,962
So you must not plan on it disappearing.

68
00:04:54,302 --> 00:05:00,311
Keeping a fork current has one dangerous
command. According to gh repo sync --help,

69
00:05:00,351 --> 00:05:06,216
a sync is a fast-forward "except when the
--force flag is specified, then the two

70
00:05:06,256 --> 00:05:11,901
branches will be synced using a hard
reset". The five answers for gh repo sync

71
00:05:11,941 --> 00:05:12,484
--force.

72
00:05:12,824 --> 00:05:17,861
It changes the destination branch of the
fork. It can destroy commits on that branch

73
00:05:17,901 --> 00:05:22,878
that the source doesn't have. Preview with
git log upstream/main..origin/main after

74
00:05:22,918 --> 00:05:26,565
a fetch. Recovery needs a clone
that still has those commits.

75
00:05:26,605 --> 00:05:31,401
It's appropriate only when the fork's
branch is meant to be an exact copy of its

76
00:05:31,441 --> 00:05:31,824
parent.

77
00:05:32,164 --> 00:05:36,706
Now the addendum, section 15.6. A star
is a bookmark and a public signal.

78
00:05:36,746 --> 00:05:41,978
It feeds rankings, in GitHub's words "many
of GitHub's repository rankings depend on

79
00:05:42,018 --> 00:05:47,062
the number of stars", and anyone can list
the stargazers of a repository they can

80
00:05:47,102 --> 00:05:52,083
read. Watching is a notification subscription.
You can narrow it to chosen event

81
00:05:52,123 --> 00:05:56,979
types such as releases and security
alerts. By default you automatically watch

82
00:05:57,019 --> 00:06:02,251
repositories you create and repositories
you are given push access to, except forks.

83
00:06:02,291 --> 00:06:05,327
An account can watch at
most 10,000 repositories.

84
00:06:05,667 --> 00:06:10,625
Neither is evidence of quality. Stars can
be manufactured: the course's research

85
00:06:10,665 --> 00:06:15,374
cites a network of more than 3,000
accounts that distributed malware through

86
00:06:15,414 --> 00:06:20,560
repositories made to look popular. And a
star count says nothing about who controls

87
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:25,809
a repository today. A useful subscription
is narrow: releases and security alerts of

88
00:06:25,849 --> 00:06:27,433
the dependencies you ship.

89
00:06:29,273 --> 00:06:33,934
The textbook's analogy: a branch office
that keeps its own ledger and uses head

90
00:06:33,974 --> 00:06:37,980
office's warehouse. Each office has
its own list of goods, its refs.

91
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:42,859
The goods, the objects, are in one building,
and anyone who knows a crate's serial

92
00:06:42,899 --> 00:06:45,001
number can ask either office for it.

93
00:06:45,341 --> 00:06:50,095
The analogy breaks when an office closes:
its list is gone and the crates remain.

94
00:06:50,435 --> 00:06:54,528
The serial number is the commit ID.
Whoever has it doesn't need the list.

95
00:06:56,368 --> 00:07:01,762
In the middle is the object database: among
others, commit bf7889c, the tip of main,

96
00:07:01,802 --> 00:07:04,608
and commit 915a81e, the
commit with the key.

97
00:07:04,948 --> 00:07:09,513
On the left, the refs of the upstream
repository: one branch, main.

98
00:07:09,553 --> 00:07:14,805
On the right, the refs of the fork: main,
and the debug branch that points at

99
00:07:14,845 --> 00:07:15,355
915a81e.

100
00:07:15,695 --> 00:07:20,689
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Copy the picture: one box of objects, two

101
00:07:20,729 --> 00:07:25,213
lists of refs. Then cross out the
fork's list, and look at what's left.

102
00:07:25,253 --> 00:07:25,850
I'll wait.

103
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:36,502
The upstream has no ref that reaches 915a81e.
But both names are doors into the same

104
00:07:36,542 --> 00:07:36,850
room.

105
00:07:37,190 --> 00:07:40,772
Crossing out the right-hand set
of refs is deleting the fork.

106
00:07:40,812 --> 00:07:42,850
The object database doesn't change.

107
00:07:44,690 --> 00:07:49,472
The platform is one bare repository,
a repository with no working tree.

108
00:07:49,512 --> 00:07:53,548
The upstream, acme-ml/prompt-registry,
is a namespace in it.

109
00:07:53,888 --> 00:07:58,522
One ref, under refs/namespaces/acme-ml/,
and 27 object files.

110
00:07:58,862 --> 00:07:59,609
Now forking.

111
00:07:59,949 --> 00:08:03,640
The fork now exists. How
many object files are there?

112
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:05,540
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

113
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:14,820
Twenty-seven before, twenty-seven
after. Forking wrote one ref.

114
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:16,313
No object was copied.

115
00:08:16,653 --> 00:08:20,345
Now the mistake. The key in this
file is a fake made for the lab.

116
00:08:20,685 --> 00:08:25,337
Note git add -f: the file was
ignored, and the flag forced it in.

117
00:08:25,377 --> 00:08:29,668
Three new objects went into the
one object database: 30 now.

118
00:08:29,708 --> 00:08:31,762
The commit ID begins 915a81e.

119
00:08:32,102 --> 00:08:37,178
Each repository lists only its own refs.
The upstream doesn't list the debug branch.

120
00:08:37,518 --> 00:08:41,595
Now a visitor who knows nothing but the
upstream repository and the commit ID.

121
00:08:41,935 --> 00:08:47,271
Quick quiz, two options. The upstream has
one branch, and it doesn't contain this

122
00:08:47,311 --> 00:08:51,585
commit. Does the fetch by ID
succeed, yes or no? Say it out loud.

123
00:09:02,425 --> 00:09:06,982
It succeeds. If you said no, you were
thinking in branches, and that's a sound

124
00:09:07,022 --> 00:09:11,756
instinct. But the upstream served a commit
that none of its refs reaches, because

125
00:09:11,796 --> 00:09:14,762
the object is in the store
it shares with the fork.

126
00:09:15,102 --> 00:09:15,983
Delete the fork.

127
00:09:16,323 --> 00:09:20,733
The fork's refs are gone. What does
cat-file -t say about the commit?

128
00:09:20,773 --> 00:09:22,087
Make your prediction.

129
00:09:31,427 --> 00:09:35,336
commit. Deleting the fork
removed refs and nothing else.

130
00:09:35,376 --> 00:09:36,958
So is the key gone? No.

131
00:09:37,298 --> 00:09:41,602
In the model, the commit dies when the
server prunes unreachable objects.

132
00:09:41,642 --> 00:09:46,184
You don't control that step on GitHub,
and no retention period is documented.

133
00:09:46,224 --> 00:09:51,183
One more caveat from Git's own manual:
namespaces "are not effective for read access

134
00:09:51,223 --> 00:09:54,813
control". The model has the same
hole as the thing it models.

135
00:09:55,153 --> 00:09:59,889
On the page of the repository you want to
fork, use the control that creates a fork,

136
00:09:59,929 --> 00:10:04,495
choose your account as the owner, and
confirm. On the new repository's page, look

137
00:10:04,535 --> 00:10:09,044
directly under its name: the page shows
the fork relation, "forked from" and the

138
00:10:09,084 --> 00:10:09,840
parent's name.

139
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:15,559
That line is the GitHub object. Clone the
fork and look for it in .git/config: you

140
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:20,978
will find remotes, and no record of the
relation. Lab 21.1 itself, the full cycle,

141
00:10:21,018 --> 00:10:26,463
runs locally in the lab shell with three
repositories and is the subject of a later

142
00:10:26,503 --> 00:10:26,860
video.

143
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:30,348
Five mistakes to watch for.

144
00:10:30,688 --> 00:10:34,096
Deleting a fork or a branch
to remove a leaked secret.

145
00:10:34,136 --> 00:10:39,396
Root cause: deletion removes refs; the
objects stay in the store the network shares

146
00:10:39,436 --> 00:10:41,120
and remain fetchable by ID.

147
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:44,975
Assuming the upstream is unaffected
by what happens in forks.

148
00:10:45,015 --> 00:10:49,871
Root cause: commits pushed to any repository
in a network can be accessible from the

149
00:10:49,911 --> 00:10:51,678
others, including the upstream.

150
00:10:52,018 --> 00:10:57,465
Looking for the fork relationship in
.git/config. Root cause: it is a GitHub object;

151
00:10:57,505 --> 00:10:59,883
the upstream remote is a convenience.

152
00:11:00,223 --> 00:11:05,295
Running gh repo sync --force on a
fork branch that has its own commits.

153
00:11:05,335 --> 00:11:10,552
Root cause: with --force the sync is
a hard reset to the parent's branch.

154
00:11:10,892 --> 00:11:15,720
Choosing a dependency by its star count.
Root cause: a star is a bookmark that can

155
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:20,053
be manufactured and says nothing about
who controls the repository today.

156
00:11:21,893 --> 00:11:26,153
Now, out of the lab. Back to the intern's
key, and the first five minutes.

157
00:11:26,193 --> 00:11:30,221
The key was published the moment the
push to the public fork finished.

158
00:11:30,261 --> 00:11:35,102
Deleting the fork changed which names list
the commit, not whether the object can be

159
00:11:35,142 --> 00:11:35,567
fetched.

160
00:11:35,907 --> 00:11:39,198
So the order of actions is
fixed. Revoke the key first.

161
00:11:39,238 --> 00:11:44,225
That's the only action that makes the leaked
value worthless, and it doesn't depend

162
00:11:44,265 --> 00:11:48,767
on anything GitHub does. Then decide
whether removal of the commit is worth

163
00:11:48,807 --> 00:11:53,794
requesting. The prevention is a secret store
and push protection, which Chapter 21B

164
00:11:53,834 --> 00:11:56,762
covers together with the
full response procedure.

165
00:11:57,102 --> 00:12:02,511
Notice what didn't help in this story: the
fact that staging.env was in .gitignore.

166
00:12:02,551 --> 00:12:04,283
The file was added with -f.

167
00:12:06,123 --> 00:12:11,964
Your turn. Do Exercise 19.4, "What a clone
receives, and where a keyword travels",

168
00:12:12,004 --> 00:12:17,773
in exercises/m19-m25-github.md. Before
each command, predict which refs and which

169
00:12:17,813 --> 00:12:23,582
objects each repository holds, and where
a piece of text in a commit message ends

170
00:12:23,622 --> 00:12:23,797
up.

171
00:12:24,137 --> 00:12:29,321
The challenge is Exercise 19.5, "The fork
that was deleted", in the same file.

172
00:12:31,161 --> 00:12:34,284
Question 241 of the CTO question bank:

173
00:12:36,624 --> 00:12:41,341
Answer out loud. A strong answer has the
three parts the question asks for, in that

174
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:45,525
order. It separates what is documented
from what is unknown, and says so.

175
00:12:45,565 --> 00:12:50,224
Its first action doesn't depend on the
platform. It closes with prevention, and it

176
00:12:50,264 --> 00:12:53,376
doesn't claim that anything
was "removed from history".

177
00:12:55,216 --> 00:12:56,143
Let's land this.

178
00:12:56,483 --> 00:12:58,143
You should now be able to say:

179
00:12:58,483 --> 00:13:03,054
A fork is its own repository with its own
refs and permissions; the network shares

180
00:13:03,094 --> 00:13:03,560
Git data.

181
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:08,144
A commit pushed anywhere in a network
can be fetched by ID through other

182
00:13:08,184 --> 00:13:11,714
repositories of the network,
even after the fork is deleted.

183
00:13:12,054 --> 00:13:16,965
Deleting a fork or a branch removes names,
not objects; no retention period for

184
00:13:17,005 --> 00:13:19,096
unreachable commits is documented.

185
00:13:19,436 --> 00:13:21,521
A leaked credential is revoked first.

186
00:13:21,861 --> 00:13:26,422
Stars are bookmarks and watching is a
subscription; neither is evidence of quality.

187
00:13:28,262 --> 00:13:32,561
Read sections 15.6 and 15.7 of Chapter 15.

188
00:13:32,901 --> 00:13:37,592
Today you watched a deleted fork leave
its commit behind, and you know the first

189
00:13:37,632 --> 00:13:40,607
move: revoke. If that still
feels unsettling, good.

190
00:13:40,647 --> 00:13:45,515
It should. Next time: issues, projects,
discussions, packages, templates and health

191
00:13:45,555 --> 00:13:49,004
files. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

192
00:13:49,044 --> 00:13:50,423
See you in the next one.
